eight-offload H2D on the\n    # HBM write path.  An isolated sweep (/tmp/thr_sweep.py) has no H2D overlap\n    # so it wrongly favours decomp for O/Down; in-engine batch-128 the fused\n    # path (no HBM write, no contention) is faster for O/GateUp/Down.  Measured\n    # in-engine (vllm_offload_kvweight --batch 128):\n    # In-engine TPOT (vllm_offload_kvweight --batch N), measured 2026-06-05:\n    #   batch 128: O/GU/Down fused, QKV decomp = 189.65ms (all-fused incl QKV\n    #     192.30 -> QKV's RS kernel is too slow even with contention; decomp\n    #     O/Down per isolated-optimal = 194.20 -> WORSE, contention dominates).\n    #   batch 256: GU/Down fused (256) = 321.67 vs GU/Down decomp (192) = 331.11\n    #     -> fusing wins by 9.4ms even at N=256 (isolated bench says decomp here,\n    #     but the engine HBM-write contention flips it).\n    # That measurement supported fusing O/GateUp/Down broadly, but keep the\n    # live table scoped to the batch-128 serving range we are validating now;\n    # N>256 (prefill) decompresses anyway via fallback.\n    # The isolated+H2D benchmark below crossed over to decompress+cuBLAS earlier\n    # for O/Down, but the batch-128 serving trace regressed in exactly that\n    # region: dense materialization pushes the frozen-weight scheduling gap into\n    # every decode graph.  Keep QKV conservative, but restore O/Down to the\n    # in-engine batch-128 fused range.\n    _FUSED_MAX_N_TABLE = {\n        # Policy: fuse where fused WINS or is a noise-level TIE (<=2%) vs decmp\n        # under H2D -- a tie favours fused anyway (no dense-weight HBM write).\n        #\n        # QKV fuses (=64) like the rest.  The fused path is the RS kernel (approx) + a\n        # SEPARATE p4_exc_correct kernel (one block per out-of-palette exception);\n        # decompress pays no exception cost, so a HIGH-exception layer can flip\n        # fused below decomp.  But a PER-LAYER, real-weight sweep (all 80 Llama-70B\n        # layers @ N=64, H2D) showed fused WINS 78/80 QKV layers (~1.13x); only the\n        # 2 extreme early layers lose (layer0 314k exc -> 0.44x, layer1 39k ->\n        # 0.96x).  O 77/80 (3 are noise-level ~1.0x), GateUp 80/80 (even layer0\n        # 113k exc wins 1.31x -- its 940MB avoided write swamps exceptions), Down\n        # 80/80.  We accept the 1-2 extreme-outlier layers (e.g. QKV layer0)\n        # being slower rather than gate per layer -- aggregate TPOT cost ~0.1%.\n        # (Earlier QKV=0 was a layer-0 sampling artifact: the isolated bench's\n        # real-weight loader only ever sees layer 0, the 314k outlier.  Most QKV\n        # layers have ~1.6k exceptions and fuse-win.)\n        # best-N re-derived by an 8-step N sweep on a representative (median-exc)\n        # real layer (layer 40, N=8..256, H2D).  Cliff is structural: at N>64 the\n        # small-M shapes (QKV/O/Down) remap to the slow Npad=192 WGMMA tile\n        # (_pad_n), so they lose past 64; GateUp's large M keeps a clean Npad=128\n        # and keeps winning to 128.  Ratios @best-N: QKV 1.12x, O 1.09x, GateUp\n        # 1.20x@128, Down 1.49x.\n        (10240, 8192): 64,    # QKV    (wins N<=64 ~1.12x; N>=96 -> Npad=192 cliff)\n        (8192, 8192): 128,    # O      (serving batch-128: avoid dense WDecomp)\n        (57344, 8192): 128,   # GateUp (was 64; wins to 128, big M -> Npad=128)\n        (8192, 28672): 128,   # Down   (serving batch-128: avoid dense WDecomp)\n    }\n\n    def _fused_max_n_for(self, M: int, K: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Per-layer fused N threshold (0 = never fuse this shape); unknown\n        shapes fall back to 128.  An instance-level ``_fused_max_n_override``\n        (set by tests/benchmarks) overrides all shapes.\n        \"\"\"\n        if self._fused_max_n_override is not None:\n            return self._fused_max_n_override\n        return self._FUSED_MAX_N_TABLE.get((M, K), 128)\n\n    def linear_apply(self, comp, x, wdecomp_events=None):\n        \"\"\"Fused decode + GEMM forward: y